{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01HNHtrNpr8iMP7sARayiR8q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# A Comprehensive History of Computing\n\n## Introduction\n\nThe history of computing is a fascinating journey that spans millennia, from ancient counting devices to modern artificial intelligence systems. Computing, at its core, is humanity's attempt to extend our cognitive abilities and solve complex problems through mechanical and electronic means. This essay explores the major milestones, key innovations, and pivotal figures that have shaped the world of computing from its earliest forms to the present day.\n\n## Ancient Computing Devices\n\nThe history of computing does not begin with electricity or even mechanics in the modern sense. Humans have sought ways to assist their calculations for thousands of years. One of the earliest known computing devices is the **Abacus**, which emerged independently in various ancient civilizations including Mesopotamia, Egypt, Persia, Greece, and China. The abacus, essentially a counting frame with beads on rods, allowed merchants and mathematicians to perform arithmetic operations with remarkable speed and accuracy. The Sumerian abacus, dating back to around 2700 BCE, used sand and pebbles arranged in columns, while the Roman abacus later refined this concept with beads on wires.\n\nAnother significant ancient computing device was **Napier's Bones**, invented by Scottish mathematician John Napier in the early 1600s. These were small rods marked with multiplication tables that could be arranged to perform multiplication and division operations. While simple by modern standards, they represented an important step toward automating mathematical calculations.\n\nThe **Antikythera mechanism**, discovered in a Greek shipwreck and dating to around 100 BCE, stands as one of the most remarkable artifacts of ancient computing. This complex device, containing at least 30 bronze gears, was apparently used to predict astronomical positions and lunar eclipses. Its sophistication was not matched again for nearly a thousand years, and it demonstrates that ancient civilizations possessed knowledge of mechanical computation far beyond what historians initially believed.\n\n## The Mechanical Age\n\nThe 17th and 18th centuries witnessed significant advances in mechanical computation. **Blaise Pascal** invented the Pascaline in 1642, a mechanical calculator capable of addition and subtraction. This device used a system of gears and wheels, and while it was cumbersome and prone to errors, it represented the first attempt at an automated general-purpose calculating machine.\n\n**Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz** improved upon Pascal's design with his **Stepped Reckoner** in 1673, which could perform all four arithmetic operations: addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division. Leibniz also made a profound contribution to computing theory by developing binary arithmetic, a concept that would become fundamental to modern digital computers centuries later.\n\nHowever, the most visionary figure of the mechanical computing age was **Charles Babbage**. In the 1820s, Babbage conceived of the **Difference Engine**, a machine designed to compute polynomial functions using the method of finite differences. His plans became increasingly elaborate, and he later envisioned the **Analytical Engine**, a more ambitious machine that would be programmable using punched cards. The Analytical Engine contained all the essential components of a modern computer: an input device (the punched cards), a processor (the \"mill\"), memory (the \"store\"), and an output device. Babbage never completed either machine during his lifetime, partly due to the manufacturing limitations of his era.\n\n**Ada Lovelace**, the only legitimate daughter of Lord Byron, became the first computer programmer by writing a detailed algorithm for the Analytical Engine. Her notes on Babbage's machine included what many consider the first computer program, and her visionary insights about the machine's potential went far beyond Babbage's own writings.\n\n## The Electrical and Electromechanical Era\n\nThe late 19th and early 20th centuries brought electricity to computing. **Herman Hollerith** developed the **tabulating machine** in 1890, which used electrical impulses to read data from punched cards. This machine was used to process data from the 1890 U.S. Census, reducing what would have taken eight years to complete in just one year. Hollerith's work laid the foundation for IBM (International Business Machines), which would become the dominant computing company for decades.\n\nThe introduction of telephone switching technology also advanced computing. Telephone systems required automated switching, and engineers applied these principles to create more sophisticated machines. These electromechanical devices represented a significant improvement over purely mechanical ones in terms of speed and reliability.\n\nDuring World War II, the need for rapid calculations in cryptography and ballistics drove computing innovation. The **Colossus** computers, developed in Britain and kept secret for decades, used vacuum tubes to perform electronic calculations. These machines were used to break the German Lorenz cipher and are credited by many historians as the first electronic digital computers. Meanwhile, in Germany, **Konrad Zuse** had designed the **Z3**, a fully programmable electromechanical computer, though it received little attention during the war.\n\n## The Electronic Computing Revolution\n\nThe post-World War II era witnessed the emergence of true electronic computers. The **ENIAC** (Electronic Numerical Integrator and Computer), completed in 1946 at the University of Pennsylvania, is often credited as the first general-purpose electronic digital computer. It weighed 30 tons, occupied 1,800 square feet, and consumed 150 kilowatts of electricity. Despite its enormous size, ENIAC could perform calculations thousands of times faster than mechanical or electromechanical devices. However, it was unreliable, with vacuum tubes frequently burning out.\n\nThe **Von Neumann architecture**, proposed by John von Neumann in 1945, became the theoretical foundation for modern computers. This architecture, which stores both programs and data in memory and retrieves them in sequence, remains the basis for virtually all computers today. Von Neumann's work provided the conceptual framework that transformed computing from machine-specific devices into programmable systems.\n\nThe development of **transistors** at Bell Laboratories in 1947 revolutionized computing. Transistors were far smaller, more reliable, and more efficient than vacuum tubes, enabling the creation of smaller, faster, and more practical computers. This innovation led to the second generation of computers in the 1950s and 1960s, which were more widely adopted and commercially viable.\n\n## The Era of Mainframes and Minicomputers\n\nThe 1950s and 1960s were dominated by large mainframe computers. Companies like IBM, Univac, and others manufactured massive machines that filled entire rooms. These computers served primarily scientific, military, and large-scale business applications. **FORTRAN**, developed by John Backus at IBM in 1957, was one of the first high-level programming languages and made programming more accessible.\n\nThe development of **integrated circuits** in the late 1950s and 1960s further accelerated progress. By combining multiple transistors and other components onto a single chip, integrated circuits enabled even greater computational power in smaller packages. **Moore's Law**, articulated by Gordon Moore in 1965, observed that the number of transistors that could fit on a chip would double approximately every two years—a trend that has largely held true for decades.\n\nThe 1960s also saw the emergence of **minicomputers**, smaller and less expensive than mainframes but more powerful than the first computers. The **Digital Equipment Corporation's PDP-8**, introduced in 1965, exemplified this trend and found widespread adoption in laboratories and smaller organizations.\n\n## The Personal Computer Revolution\n\nThe 1970s and 1980s witnessed a dramatic democratization of computing through the personal computer (PC) revolution. Innovations in microprocessors—single chips containing the computing power of earlier computers—made affordable personal computers possible.\n\n**Apple Computer**, founded by Steve Jobs and Steve Wozniak, introduced the **Apple II** in 1977, one of the first commercial personal computers with color graphics and sound. **IBM** entered the personal computer market in 1981 with the **IBM PC**, which used the Intel 8086 processor and the MS-DOS operating system. IBM's entry into the market legitimized personal computers for business use and established industry standards that persist today.\n\n**Microsoft**, founded by Bill Gates and Paul Allen, achieved phenomenal success by developing operating systems and software for personal computers. The company's **DOS** and later **Windows** operating systems became ubiquitous. **Apple's Macintosh**, introduced in 1984, popularized the graphical user interface (GUI) for personal computers, making them more user-friendly.\n\nThe 1980s also saw the development of **local area networks (LANs)** and the increased interconnection of computers. These technologies laid the groundwork for modern networking.\n\n## The Internet Era and Beyond\n\nThe development of the **Internet** in the 1960s, building on government research (ARPANET), gradually transformed computing in the late 20th century. The **World Wide Web**, invented by Tim Berners-Lee in 1989, provided a user-friendly interface to the Internet and catalyzed its explosive growth in the 1990s.\n\nThe 1990s and 2000s witnessed the rise of the **World Wide Web** and **e-commerce**. Companies like **Amazon** and **Google** emerged as computational giants. Google's innovative approach to search, based on sophisticated algorithms and massive distributed computing infrastructure, demonstrated the power of computing at scale.\n\nThe early 21st century brought several transformative technologies:\n\n**Cloud Computing** emerged as companies moved from owning and maintaining their own servers to utilizing shared computational resources hosted by providers like Amazon Web Services (AWS), Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud Platform. This shift democratized access to massive computing resources.\n\n**Mobile Computing** exploded with the introduction of smartphones. The **iPhone**, released by Apple in 2007, and the subsequent proliferation of Android devices revolutionized personal computing, making powerful computers accessible in people's pockets.\n\n**Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning** have undergone a renaissance driven by deep learning, large datasets, and powerful GPUs. Systems like ChatGPT, image generation models, and autonomous vehicles demonstrate the remarkable capabilities of modern AI.\n\n## Computing Paradigms and Theoretical Foundations\n\nThroughout the history of computing, theoretical advances have been as important as hardware innovations. **Alan Turing's** concept of the **Turing Machine** (1936) provided a mathematical foundation for understanding computation itself. Turing proved fundamental limits on what could be computed, establishing that some problems are inherently unsolvable.\n\n**Claude Shannon's** work on **information theory** provided the theoretical underpinnings for understanding data, communication, and computation. His insights established the bit as the fundamental unit of information.\n\nThe development of **algorithms** and **computational complexity theory** in the latter 20th century provided frameworks for understanding the efficiency of computations and the inherent difficulty of various problems.\n\n## Operating Systems and Software\n\nThe evolution of **operating systems** deserves special mention. Early computers required operators to physically rewire machines for different tasks. Operating systems emerged as software that managed hardware resources and allowed multiple users or programs to access the computer. **Unix**, developed at Bell Labs, became influential for its elegant design and portability. **Linux**, an open-source Unix-like operating system, has become ubiquitous in servers, supercomputers, and smartphones.\n\nThe development of **programming languages** from machine code to FORTRAN to C to Python reflects increasing abstraction and accessibility. Each generation of languages made programming more intuitive and productive.\n\n## Modern Challenges and Future Directions\n\nContemporary computing faces several grand challenges. **Quantum computing** promises to solve certain classes of problems far faster than classical computers, though practical quantum computers remain in their infancy. **Energy efficiency** and the environmental impact of computing infrastructure have become increasingly important concerns.\n\n**Cybersecurity** has emerged as a critical field as our dependence on computing systems has grown. **Artificial intelligence** raises profound questions about the future of work, autonomy, and the nature of intelligence itself.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe history of computing represents one of humanity's greatest intellectual and engineering achievements. From the abacus to quantum computers, from mechanical calculators to artificial neural networks, computing has evolved at an extraordinary pace. Each generation of innovators built upon the work of their predecessors, creating exponential improvements in speed, power, and accessibility.\n\nWhat is remarkable is that the fundamental concepts underlying computing—logic, algorithms, and information processing—remain recognizable from the time of Babbage and Lovelace. The transformation has been primarily one of scale and implementation, not radical reimagining of core principles.\n\nAs we look toward the future, computing will likely remain at the center of human innovation. Whether through quantum computing, biological computing, or technologies yet to be imagined, the quest to extend our cognitive capabilities through mechanical and electronic means continues. The history of computing is not merely a chronicle of past achievements but a prologue to a future where computational thinking and artificial intelligence will likely play increasingly central roles in addressing humanity's greatest challenges.\n\nThe journey from pebbles arranged in sand to artificial intelligence systems that can engage in complex reasoning demonstrates the remarkable trajectory of computing history—a trajectory that shows no signs of slowing."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":15550,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4c805af4-e730-47b4-9b9b-0bffb2483b20","uuid":"4d52a234-b103-49e2-a448-72da0cf7e3aa","timestamp":"2026-03-05T18:47:43.788Z"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":28986,"duration_api_ms":43931,"num_turns":1,"result":"# A Comprehensive History of Computing\n\n## Introduction\n\nThe history of computing is a fascinating journey that spans millennia, from ancient counting devices to modern artificial intelligence systems. Computing, at its core, is humanity's attempt to extend our cognitive abilities and solve complex problems through mechanical and electronic means. This essay explores the major milestones, key innovations, and pivotal figures that have shaped the world of computing from its earliest forms to the present day.\n\n## Ancient Computing Devices\n\nThe history of computing does not begin with electricity or even mechanics in the modern sense. Humans have sought ways to assist their calculations for thousands of years. One of the earliest known computing devices is the **Abacus**, which emerged independently in various ancient civilizations including Mesopotamia, Egypt, Persia, Greece, and China. The abacus, essentially a counting frame with beads on rods, allowed merchants and mathematicians to perform arithmetic operations with remarkable speed and accuracy. The Sumerian abacus, dating back to around 2700 BCE, used sand and pebbles arranged in columns, while the Roman abacus later refined this concept with beads on wires.\n\nAnother significant ancient computing device was **Napier's Bones**, invented by Scottish mathematician John Napier in the early 1600s. These were small rods marked with multiplication tables that could be arranged to perform multiplication and division operations. While simple by modern standards, they represented an important step toward automating mathematical calculations.\n\nThe **Antikythera mechanism**, discovered in a Greek shipwreck and dating to around 100 BCE, stands as one of the most remarkable artifacts of ancient computing. This complex device, containing at least 30 bronze gears, was apparently used to predict astronomical positions and lunar eclipses. Its sophistication was not matched again for nearly a thousand years, and it demonstrates that ancient civilizations possessed knowledge of mechanical computation far beyond what historians initially believed.\n\n## The Mechanical Age\n\nThe 17th and 18th centuries witnessed significant advances in mechanical computation. **Blaise Pascal** invented the Pascaline in 1642, a mechanical calculator capable of addition and subtraction. This device used a system of gears and wheels, and while it was cumbersome and prone to errors, it represented the first attempt at an automated general-purpose calculating machine.\n\n**Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz** improved upon Pascal's design with his **Stepped Reckoner** in 1673, which could perform all four arithmetic operations: addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division. Leibniz also made a profound contribution to computing theory by developing binary arithmetic, a concept that would become fundamental to modern digital computers centuries later.\n\nHowever, the most visionary figure of the mechanical computing age was **Charles Babbage**. In the 1820s, Babbage conceived of the **Difference Engine**, a machine designed to compute polynomial functions using the method of finite differences. His plans became increasingly elaborate, and he later envisioned the **Analytical Engine**, a more ambitious machine that would be programmable using punched cards. The Analytical Engine contained all the essential components of a modern computer: an input device (the punched cards), a processor (the \"mill\"), memory (the \"store\"), and an output device. Babbage never completed either machine during his lifetime, partly due to the manufacturing limitations of his era.\n\n**Ada Lovelace**, the only legitimate daughter of Lord Byron, became the first computer programmer by writing a detailed algorithm for the Analytical Engine. Her notes on Babbage's machine included what many consider the first computer program, and her visionary insights about the machine's potential went far beyond Babbage's own writings.\n\n## The Electrical and Electromechanical Era\n\nThe late 19th and early 20th centuries brought electricity to computing. **Herman Hollerith** developed the **tabulating machine** in 1890, which used electrical impulses to read data from punched cards. This machine was used to process data from the 1890 U.S. Census, reducing what would have taken eight years to complete in just one year. Hollerith's work laid the foundation for IBM (International Business Machines), which would become the dominant computing company for decades.\n\nThe introduction of telephone switching technology also advanced computing. Telephone systems required automated switching, and engineers applied these principles to create more sophisticated machines. These electromechanical devices represented a significant improvement over purely mechanical ones in terms of speed and reliability.\n\nDuring World War II, the need for rapid calculations in cryptography and ballistics drove computing innovation. The **Colossus** computers, developed in Britain and kept secret for decades, used vacuum tubes to perform electronic calculations. These machines were used to break the German Lorenz cipher and are credited by many historians as the first electronic digital computers. Meanwhile, in Germany, **Konrad Zuse** had designed the **Z3**, a fully programmable electromechanical computer, though it received little attention during the war.\n\n## The Electronic Computing Revolution\n\nThe post-World War II era witnessed the emergence of true electronic computers. The **ENIAC** (Electronic Numerical Integrator and Computer), completed in 1946 at the University of Pennsylvania, is often credited as the first general-purpose electronic digital computer. It weighed 30 tons, occupied 1,800 square feet, and consumed 150 kilowatts of electricity. Despite its enormous size, ENIAC could perform calculations thousands of times faster than mechanical or electromechanical devices. However, it was unreliable, with vacuum tubes frequently burning out.\n\nThe **Von Neumann architecture**, proposed by John von Neumann in 1945, became the theoretical foundation for modern computers. This architecture, which stores both programs and data in memory and retrieves them in sequence, remains the basis for virtually all computers today. Von Neumann's work provided the conceptual framework that transformed computing from machine-specific devices into programmable systems.\n\nThe development of **transistors** at Bell Laboratories in 1947 revolutionized computing. Transistors were far smaller, more reliable, and more efficient than vacuum tubes, enabling the creation of smaller, faster, and more practical computers. This innovation led to the second generation of computers in the 1950s and 1960s, which were more widely adopted and commercially viable.\n\n## The Era of Mainframes and Minicomputers\n\nThe 1950s and 1960s were dominated by large mainframe computers. Companies like IBM, Univac, and others manufactured massive machines that filled entire rooms. These computers served primarily scientific, military, and large-scale business applications. **FORTRAN**, developed by John Backus at IBM in 1957, was one of the first high-level programming languages and made programming more accessible.\n\nThe development of **integrated circuits** in the late 1950s and 1960s further accelerated progress. By combining multiple transistors and other components onto a single chip, integrated circuits enabled even greater computational power in smaller packages. **Moore's Law**, articulated by Gordon Moore in 1965, observed that the number of transistors that could fit on a chip would double approximately every two years—a trend that has largely held true for decades.\n\nThe 1960s also saw the emergence of **minicomputers**, smaller and less expensive than mainframes but more powerful than the first computers. The **Digital Equipment Corporation's PDP-8**, introduced in 1965, exemplified this trend and found widespread adoption in laboratories and smaller organizations.\n\n## The Personal Computer Revolution\n\nThe 1970s and 1980s witnessed a dramatic democratization of computing through the personal computer (PC) revolution. Innovations in microprocessors—single chips containing the computing power of earlier computers—made affordable personal computers possible.\n\n**Apple Computer**, founded by Steve Jobs and Steve Wozniak, introduced the **Apple II** in 1977, one of the first commercial personal computers with color graphics and sound. **IBM** entered the personal computer market in 1981 with the **IBM PC**, which used the Intel 8086 processor and the MS-DOS operating system. IBM's entry into the market legitimized personal computers for business use and established industry standards that persist today.\n\n**Microsoft**, founded by Bill Gates and Paul Allen, achieved phenomenal success by developing operating systems and software for personal computers. The company's **DOS** and later **Windows** operating systems became ubiquitous. **Apple's Macintosh**, introduced in 1984, popularized the graphical user interface (GUI) for personal computers, making them more user-friendly.\n\nThe 1980s also saw the development of **local area networks (LANs)** and the increased interconnection of computers. These technologies laid the groundwork for modern networking.\n\n## The Internet Era and Beyond\n\nThe development of the **Internet** in the 1960s, building on government research (ARPANET), gradually transformed computing in the late 20th century. The **World Wide Web**, invented by Tim Berners-Lee in 1989, provided a user-friendly interface to the Internet and catalyzed its explosive growth in the 1990s.\n\nThe 1990s and 2000s witnessed the rise of the **World Wide Web** and **e-commerce**. Companies like **Amazon** and **Google** emerged as computational giants. Google's innovative approach to search, based on sophisticated algorithms and massive distributed computing infrastructure, demonstrated the power of computing at scale.\n\nThe early 21st century brought several transformative technologies:\n\n**Cloud Computing** emerged as companies moved from owning and maintaining their own servers to utilizing shared computational resources hosted by providers like Amazon Web Services (AWS), Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud Platform. This shift democratized access to massive computing resources.\n\n**Mobile Computing** exploded with the introduction of smartphones. The **iPhone**, released by Apple in 2007, and the subsequent proliferation of Android devices revolutionized personal computing, making powerful computers accessible in people's pockets.\n\n**Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning** have undergone a renaissance driven by deep learning, large datasets, and powerful GPUs. Systems like ChatGPT, image generation models, and autonomous vehicles demonstrate the remarkable capabilities of modern AI.\n\n## Computing Paradigms and Theoretical Foundations\n\nThroughout the history of computing, theoretical advances have been as important as hardware innovations. **Alan Turing's** concept of the **Turing Machine** (1936) provided a mathematical foundation for understanding computation itself. Turing proved fundamental limits on what could be computed, establishing that some problems are inherently unsolvable.\n\n**Claude Shannon's** work on **information theory** provided the theoretical underpinnings for understanding data, communication, and computation. His insights established the bit as the fundamental unit of information.\n\nThe development of **algorithms** and **computational complexity theory** in the latter 20th century provided frameworks for understanding the efficiency of computations and the inherent difficulty of various problems.\n\n## Operating Systems and Software\n\nThe evolution of **operating systems** deserves special mention. Early computers required operators to physically rewire machines for different tasks. Operating systems emerged as software that managed hardware resources and allowed multiple users or programs to access the computer. **Unix**, developed at Bell Labs, became influential for its elegant design and portability. **Linux**, an open-source Unix-like operating system, has become ubiquitous in servers, supercomputers, and smartphones.\n\nThe development of **programming languages** from machine code to FORTRAN to C to Python reflects increasing abstraction and accessibility. Each generation of languages made programming more intuitive and productive.\n\n## Modern Challenges and Future Directions\n\nContemporary computing faces several grand challenges. **Quantum computing** promises to solve certain classes of problems far faster than classical computers, though practical quantum computers remain in their infancy. **Energy efficiency** and the environmental impact of computing infrastructure have become increasingly important concerns.\n\n**Cybersecurity** has emerged as a critical field as our dependence on computing systems has grown. **Artificial intelligence** raises profound questions about the future of work, autonomy, and the nature of intelligence itself.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe history of computing represents one of humanity's greatest intellectual and engineering achievements. From the abacus to quantum computers, from mechanical calculators to artificial neural networks, computing has evolved at an extraordinary pace. Each generation of innovators built upon the work of their predecessors, creating exponential improvements in speed, power, and accessibility.\n\nWhat is remarkable is that the fundamental concepts underlying computing—logic, algorithms, and information processing—remain recognizable from the time of Babbage and Lovelace. The transformation has been primarily one of scale and implementation, not radical reimagining of core principles.\n\nAs we look toward the future, computing will likely remain at the center of human innovation. Whether through quantum computing, biological computing, or technologies yet to be imagined, the quest to extend our cognitive capabilities through mechanical and electronic means continues. The history of computing is not merely a chronicle of past achievements but a prologue to a future where computational thinking and artificial intelligence will likely play increasingly central roles in addressing humanity's greatest challenges.\n\nThe journey from pebbles arranged in sand to artificial intelligence systems that can engage in complex reasoning demonstrates the remarkable trajectory of computing history—a trajectory that shows no signs of slowing.","session_id":"4c805af4-e730-47b4-9b9b-0bffb2483b20","total_cost_usd":0.07521905,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":15550,"output_tokens":2871,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0}},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-4-5-20251101":{"inputTokens":4092,"outputTokens":518,"cacheReadInputTokens":21030,"cacheCreationInputTokens":1876,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.05565,"contextWindow":200000},"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":3,"outputTokens":179,"cacheReadInputTokens":9743,"cacheCreationInputTokens":1427,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.00365605,"contextWindow":200000},"claude-haiku-4-5":{"inputTokens":3,"outputTokens":2871,"cacheReadInputTokens":15550,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.015913,"contextWindow":200000}},"permission_denials":[],"uuid":"052f4b93-2207-4b19-82cb-f641ed489439","timestamp":"2026-03-05T18:47:44.028Z"}
